{
 "the_question": "A laboratory can synthesise five of the compounds it is considering. Which five? This measures the only thing that matters for that decision: how many of the genuinely best five end up in the five we nominate.",
 "definition": "For each target, rank the held-out compounds by measured affinity and by predicted affinity. Count how many of the true best five appear in the predicted best five. Targets with fewer than ten scored compounds are excluded because a top five out of nine is not a selection.",
 "n_targets": 119,
 "mean_hits_of_five": 2.87,
 "ci95": [
  2.66,
  3.08
 ],
 "random_baseline": 1.21,
 "enrichment_over_random": 2.4,
 "distribution": {
  "0": 4,
  "1": 8,
  "2": 33,
  "3": 39,
  "4": 25,
  "5": 10
 },
 "at_least_one": "115 of 119",
 "at_least_two": "107 of 119",
 "caveats_that_belong_next_to_this": [
  "The held-out sets are built to spread evenly across the affinity range, so the true best five are separable by construction. A real campaign's compounds are often variations on one idea and may be far closer together in potency, which would make this harder. This number is not a promise about an arbitrary compound list.",
  "These sets were built under the clustering rule later found to be weaker than documented, which let members of one chemical series into a set. The corpus is being re-measured under the corrected rule and this figure should be recomputed when that finishes.",
  "Retrospective, like everything else here. No compound was selected by this method and then made.",
  "The random baseline is the hypergeometric expectation, 25 divided by the set size, averaged over targets. It is what blind picking would give on these same sets."
 ],
